      "writtenOn": "2024-07-10T23:47:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "AVRCP has four scopes:\n- VFS (\"file system\" but pretty much everything under a given player)\n- Player (really also the filesystem, but this is just the top level of a device, which lists all the players that exist. Its added to our tree)\n- Now Playing (the now playing list, not part of our tree)\n- Search (the most recent search results, not in the tree, nor do we support this)\n\nThis function used to just be `notifyChanged(BrowseTree.BrowseNode node)`, where we could take any node. We would then say \"if the scope is now playing, actually treat this as a queue, else its part of our tree so notify on it\"\n\nNow, I split the queue off into its own function (it reads better, and its safer because its explicit) and made the rest of the functionality for our tree this function, `onBrowseNodeChanged()`.\n\nThus, we don\u0027t need the now playing scoped nodes as they go elsewhere. We also don\u0027t need the search scope nodes, because we don\u0027t support those at all. They would _also_ deserve their own function though and wouldn\u0027t go here.\n\nWhats left is just the Player list and VFS, which together make up the entirety of our browse tree for a device.\n\nSo, short answer, this is strengthening and clarity by being explicit about what we were already receiving and handling.",
